latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Iterate through the grid and count occurrences of each non-black color.\n   b. For each color, record its leftmost, rightmost, topmost, and bottommost positions.\n   c. Calculate the horizontal and vertical span for each color.\n   d. Determine the center position for each color.\n\n2. Rank the colors:\n   a. Sort the colors based on their occurrence count (descending order).\n   b. If counts are equal, use the topmost position as a secondary sorting criterion.\n   c. Select the top two colors from this sorted list.\n\n3. Determine vertical positioning in the output grid:\n   a. For each of the two selected colors, calculate its vertical position in the 3x3 output grid based on its topmost position in the input grid.\n   b. Ensure that if both colors have the same calculated position, the second color is placed one row below (if possible).\n\n4. Determine horizontal representation:\n   a. For each color, decide whether to represent it with one or two horizontal cells based on its horizontal span in the input grid.\n   b. If a color spans more than half the input grid width, represent it with two cells; otherwise, use one cell.\n\n5. Create the output grid:\n   a. Initialize a 3x3 grid filled with black (0).\n   b. Place the first color in its calculated vertical position, starting from the leftmost column.\n   c. Place the second color in its calculated vertical position, also starting from the leftmost column.\n   d. Extend colors horizontally if determined in step 4.\n\n6. Adjust the output grid:\n   a. Ensure that no color occupies more than two rows.\n   b. If a color is represented in two cells horizontally and doesn't span two rows vertically, reduce it to one cell.\n   c. Shift all colored cells upward if the top row is entirely black and there are colored cells below.\n\n7. Fill remaining cells:\n   a. Ensure all unassigned cells are filled with black (0).\n\n8. Create and return the final ColoredGrid object with the output values.\n\nThis plan takes into account the need to preserve vertical ordering, represent horizontal span, ensure minimal representation of both prominent colors, and handle edge cases like identical vertical positioning or dominant color presence. It should be flexible enough to handle all the observed test cases and potential variations within the problem constraints."
